 ### [Grid-based Convection-Diffusion Illumination](19655-grid-based-convection-diffusion-illumination.md)
-Grid-based Convection-Diffusion Illumination is a real-time volume rendering technique that models light propagation, absorption, and multiple scattering within participating media by solving a unified partial differential equation (PDE) on a regular grid.
+Grid-based Convection-Diffusion Illumination is a real-time volume rendering technique that models light propagation, absorption, and multiple scattering within participating media by solving a unified partial differential equation (PDE) on a regular grid. It distinguishes itself from Monte Carlo ray tracing by separating illumination modeling from simulation, allowing the use of highly parallelized numerical schemes for real-time performance, and from Summed Area Table (SAT) techniques by producing realistic multiple scattering effects rather than just global shadows. It is chosen when real-time performance on regular grids is required and multiple scattering effects are desired over simple shadowing.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The precomputation step is performed once per frame or scene, making it independent of the number of pixels [S1, unofficial]. The ray tracing step is linear with respect to the number of volume elements along the ray.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
